.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMM-STOP-MODULE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emm-stop-module \- stop a euclid module and keep it stopped
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B emm stop-module
.BR \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Stops every instance of a module and keeps them stopped.
.PP
This records
.I desired state
rather than issuing a one-off stop, and that is the difference that
matters. An instance that is merely stopped is one the manager brings
straight back: crashed instances are restarted, the autoscaler spawns up
to the module's minimum, and a manager restart starts everything the
configuration declares. A module stopped here is left alone by all
three, and stays down until
.BR euclid-cli-emm-start-module (1)
brings it back \- including across a restart of
.IR euclid-mgr .
.PP
The instances are stopped on the manager's next reconcile tick, within a
few seconds, not at the moment the command returns. The module's pool
slots are kept, marked stopped, so
.B start-module
brings the module back as it was rather than having to remember how big
the pool used to be.
.SH BEFORE YOU STOP SOMETHING
.TP
.B Requests fail while it is down
Nothing queues them and nothing routes around them: a request for a
stopped module has no instance to go to.
.TP
.B Dependants will not start
A module that names the stopped one in its
.I dependencies
waits for it, so stopping something in the middle of the dependency
graph keeps everything below it from coming up at the next manager
start. Check
.I euclid.json
before stopping a module others are declared to depend on.
.TP
.B Applications keep running
An application is not held back by a stopped module the way it is held
back by one that has not come up yet \- a module nobody wants running is
not one to wait for.
.SH WHAT CANNOT BE STOPPED
.TP
.B emm
refuses to stop itself. There would be nothing left to start it again,
and a door that locks behind you is worse than an error.
.TP
.B Applications and transfer servers
are modules as far as the manager is concerned, but their desired state
belongs to EAP and ETS and is reconciled from there, so anything
recorded here would be undone within seconds. Use
.B eap stop-application
or
.B ets stop-server
for those; this command refuses them rather than pretending.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-module " " \fIMODULE\fR
Module to stop, e.g. \fIftp\fR. Required. Must be a module the manager
knows about \- an unknown name is rejected with
.I "HTTP 404"
rather than silently recorded.
.SH OUTPUT
The JSON response is printed to standard output:
.RS
.TP
.I name
The module that was stopped.
.TP
.I stopped
.I true
\- what is now recorded for it.
.TP
.I runningInstances
How many instances the pool held when the request was answered. The
manager stops them on its next reconcile, so immediately afterwards this
is still the count from before.
.RE
.PP
Stopping a module that is already stopped changes nothing, answers the
same way, and is not an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The module is recorded as stopped.
.TP
.B 1
The stored session is missing or not an administrator's, the module is
unknown, it is
.I emm
itself or not a euclid module, the request could not be sent, or the
server rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Stop the FTP transfer front end:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm stop-module --module ftp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Confirm it is down, then bring it back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm list-modules

euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 emm start-module --module ftp
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-start-module (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-list-modules (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emm-set-instances (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
